  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-78",
              "name": "OS Command Injection in POST /api/ping host parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "OS Command Injection in POST /api/ping host parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The **`host`** field of `POST /api/ping` is interpolated unsanitized into a shell command string passed to `child_process.exec`, allowing an unauthenticated client to execute arbitrary OS commands on the server and read their output directly in the HTTP response."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The **`host`** field of `POST /api/ping` is interpolated unsanitized into a shell command string passed to `child_process.exec`, allowing an unauthenticated client to execute arbitrary OS commands on the server and read their output directly in the HTTP response.\n\nAny network-reachable client achieves arbitrary command execution as the service user (demonstrated: `id` returned `uid=1001(pentester) gid=1001(pentester) groups=1001(pentester),27(sudo)`, plus a unique marker echoed in-band). This grants full control of the host under the application's privileges: arbitrary file read/write (e.g. `cat /etc/passwd`, credential theft, key planting), service disruption, and persistence. No authentication or user interaction is required.\n\nAvoid shell interpretation entirely: use `child_process.execFile` (or `spawn` without `shell: true`) and pass the host as a single array element, so attacker input can never be parsed as shell syntax — this is the applied fix. Additionally append a `--` argument terminator before the user value so a hostile host cannot be interpreted as a `ping` option, and consider validating `host` against a hostname/IP pattern before use as defense in depth. Never build shell command strings by interpolating request data into template literals passed to `exec`.",
                "markdown": "The **`host`** field of `POST /api/ping` is interpolated unsanitized into a shell command string passed to `child_process.exec`, allowing an unauthenticated client to execute arbitrary OS commands on the server and read their output directly in the HTTP response.\n\nAny network-reachable client achieves arbitrary command execution as the service user (demonstrated: `id` returned `uid=1001(pentester) gid=1001(pentester) groups=1001(pentester),27(sudo)`, plus a unique marker echoed in-band). This grants full control of the host under the application's privileges: arbitrary file read/write (e.g. `cat /etc/passwd`, credential theft, key planting), service disruption, and persistence. No authentication or user interaction is required.\n\nAvoid shell interpretation entirely: use `child_process.execFile` (or `spawn` without `shell: true`) and pass the host as a single array element, so attacker input can never be parsed as shell syntax — this is the applied fix. Additionally append a `--` argument terminator before the user value so a hostile host cannot be interpreted as a `ping` option, and consider validating `host` against a hostname/IP pattern before use as defense in depth. Never build shell command strings by interpolating request data into template literals passed to `exec`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-78",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.7.0"
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-78",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "OS Command Injection in POST /api/ping host parameter\n\nThe **`host`** field of `POST /api/ping` is interpolated unsanitized into a shell command string passed to `child_process.exec`, allowing an unauthenticated client to execute arbitrary OS commands on the server and read their output directly in the HTTP response."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
