          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "34021188d4132f6469f848c231d9054514a9f6768fb7ce80a03c17f1bed6613c"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "9.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "12c6db8d05ce36b63d97441c58bd0dc6c7081ec04fd9309c5b5fd83b0f6605fd",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "critical",
              "cvss": 9.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-13 07:59:55 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/strix-test-app",
              "endpoint": "/api/ping",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-78",
              "impact": "Any network-reachable client achieves arbitrary command execution as the service user (demonstrated: `id` returned `uid=1001(pentester) gid=1001(pentester) groups=1001(pentester),27(sudo)`, plus a unique marker echoed in-band). This grants full control of the host under the application's privileges: arbitrary file read/write (e.g. `cat /etc/passwd`, credential theft, key planting), service disruption, and persistence. No authentication or user interaction is required.",
              "technical_analysis": "The handler at `server.js:8-13` reads the attacker-controlled JSON field `req.body.host` (defaulting to `127.0.0.1`) and interpolates it into a template literal passed to `child_process.exec`:\n\n```javascript title=server.js\napp.post('/api/ping', (req, res) => {\n    const host = req.body.host || '127.0.0.1';\n    exec(`ping -c 1 ${host}`, (err, stdout) => {\n        res.json({ output: stdout, error: err ? err.message : null });\n    });\n});\n```\n\n`child_process.exec` runs its command through `/bin/sh -c`, so every shell metacharacter in `host` is interpreted by the shell: `;` terminates the ping and starts a new command, `&&` chains on success, and backticks / `$(...)` are substituted in place. No validation, allowlist, or escaping exists anywhere on the path, and the resulting `stdout` is returned to the client in the JSON response, giving in-band output exfiltration. Four payload classes were verified live (`;`, `&&`, backticks, `$(...)`), each executing `id` as the service user.",
              "remediation_steps": "Avoid shell interpretation entirely: use `child_process.execFile` (or `spawn` without `shell: true`) and pass the host as a single array element, so attacker input can never be parsed as shell syntax — this is the applied fix. Additionally append a `--` argument terminator before the user value so a hostile host cannot be interpreted as a `ping` option, and consider validating `host` against a hostname/IP pattern before use as defense in depth. Never build shell command strings by interpolating request data into template literals passed to `exec`.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively looked for controls that could neutralize this: no input validation, allowlist, or escaping exists anywhere on the `req.body.host` → `exec()` path; the route is unauthenticated with no rate limiting or WAF. All four metacharacter classes (`;`, `&&`, backticks, `$(...)`) were tested and each executes, so no payload-specific filtering is in effect. Negative control performed: after replacing `exec` with argument-array `execFile` and restarting the identical service, the same payloads fail (host string resolved literally) while benign hosts still succeed — confirming shell interpretation via the template literal is the root cause and the fix closes it. The only exploitation constraint is network reachability of port 3456.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the service is reachable only from localhost by trusted operators (not bound for external clients) would lower exploitability and severity; no evidence can raise it further, since unauthenticated arbitrary command execution with in-band output is already demonstrated.",
              "fix_verification": "Gate 1 (Applicability): the diff contains only the two changed lines (import and exec call); `fix_before` blocks are verbatim copies of the original source; `node --check server.js` passes on the patched file. Gate 2 (Security closure): re-ran the original PoC payloads against the restarted patched service — `; id; echo <marker>`, `&& id`, `` `id` ``, and `$(id)` all now fail with the literal string passed to ping (`ping: 127.0.0.1; id; echo ...: Name or service not known`) and no command output; shell interpretation is gone because `execFile` with an argument array never invokes `/bin/sh`. Gate 3 (Bypass review): tested alternate input classes against the patched code — array-valued `host` (coerced to a single string argument, no shell), object-valued `host` (`[object Object]` as hostname), embedded whitespace/extra flags (`127.0.0.1 -c 10` treated as one literal hostname; the `--` terminator prevents leading-dash option injection), and a null byte (500 `ERR_INVALID_ARG_VALUE`, identical to pre-patch `exec` behavior — verified `exec` also throws on null bytes via a direct Node check, so not a regression); the service survived all of these and continued serving. Gate 4 (Preserved behavior): benign `127.0.0.1`, `localhost`, omitted-host default (`127.0.0.1`), and `GET /` all still work with identical output. Gate 5 (Repository checks): `node --check server.js` OK; the repo defines no runnable test suite (`npm test` is a placeholder), so runtime verification was performed manually via HTTP against the running service. Gap: no automated tests exist to add a regression case to; the manual before/after transcripts above serve as the verification record.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "Start the service (`node server.js` in the app directory; listens on port 3456). Send a baseline benign request to `POST /api/ping` with body `{\"host\":\"127.0.0.1\"}` and observe normal ping output. Then send the exploit request with body `{\"host\":\"127.0.0.1; id; echo STRIXd0826e957a31\"}` — the response's `output` field contains the `id` result and the marker after the ping output, proving injected command execution with in-band output exfiltration. Repeat with `&&`, backtick, and `$(...)` payload classes to confirm each executes.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ],
